3. AGENT FRAMEWORK / OSS FRONTIER NEW =E2=80=94 Paper2Agent

*What is new:* automated conversion of scientific papers into AI agents.
Published in Nature on Sep 17, 2026.

*Technical delta:* instead of an agent merely retrieving/summarizing a
paper, the research artifact is transformed into an interactive agent that
exposes the paper=E2=80=99s methods/data/code as usable capability. The pub=
lication
also describes paper-agents interacting to support discovery.

*Why serious builders may use it:* scientific tooling has unusually high
friction between reading a method and actually running/adapting it.
Packaging the method as an agent attacks that integration bottleneck
directly.

*Route:* Nature paper: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11044-y .
Code/reproduction status should be rechecked next run rather than inferred
from secondary snippets.

*Trajectory inference:* 6m: reproductions and domain-specific paper agents;
18m: agent-callable research-method registries; 36m: papers increasingly
ship executable agent interfaces; 60m: scientific literature partly behaves
like a capability graph rather than a text corpus.

*Privately implication:* a paper-agent is also a software supply-chain
object. If scientific methods become callable agents, Privately should
eventually be able to fingerprint the code/data/tools/network/authority a
paper-agent introduces before allowing it into a research environment.

*Decision:* SANDBOX / WATCH.

*Minimum experiment:* take one open paper with an existing repository,
convert it through the framework when code is available, then measure
whether a second agent can use the resulting interface without bespoke
integration. Falsifier: conversion produces little beyond RAG/wrapper
behavior and cannot reliably expose the actual computational method.
